nt safety-tuned checkpoint (confounds base capability) vs external filter wrapper (keeps base model constant)", "Precise low/mid/high attacker-budget calibration per family and the cross-family comparability convention", "Exact dataset list and item counts (AdvBench vs JBB vs HarmBench; full vs subsampled items; category balance)", "Judge-model choice and rubric strictness; avoiding judge = attacker/target model-family bias", "Whether full-factorial size is mandatory or may be downsized for feasibility (GPU disabled; design-only deliverable)", "Acceptable seed-replication count R given cost constraints"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-d83e7a3b7ebb43e6a1b2eb062057c10a", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-d83e7a3b7ebb43e6a1b2eb062057c10a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "plan", "answer": "PROPOSED APPROACH (stage=plan; not the finished protocol)\n\n## 0. Source material found\n- Workspace contains only `/workspace/task.json` (question + context + \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\" in `documents.track.md`). There is **no physical track.md / design doc / datasets / reference implementation** present, no ML packages installed, network disabled, no GPU. This is a design-only deliverable \u2014 nothing to run. So the only fixed source facts are the ones you stated: token-level attacker = **AutoDAN**, prompt-level attacker = **PAIR**, outcome = **ASR**, measured **across datasets and attack styles**, with attacker strength, defense strength, and \"previously tested tricks\" as the manipulated axes.\n\n## 1. Fixed facts to build on\n- AutoDAN (Liu et al. 2023): **token-level** \u2014 a genetic algorithm optimizes an adversarial *suffix* (token sequence appended to a seed prompt) to produce stealthy jailbreak prompts; strength is naturally a function of genetic search budget (population \u00d7 generations) and per-candidate target evaluations.\n- PAIR (Chao et al. 2023): **prompt-level** \u2014 an attacker LLM iteratively refines a *whole prompt* using the target's responses and an attacker-side judge; strength is naturally a function of the number of refinement iterations / target+attacker query budget.\n- Both were benchmarked on harmful-behavior sets from the **AdvBench** family; downstream suites (JailbreakBench JBB-Behaviors, HarmBench) are natural dataset candidates with their own evaluation conventions.\n\n## 2. Factorial design skeleton (ASR as outcome, crossed over datasets & attack styles)\nFactors:\n- **A \u2014 Attack style** (2): Token-level / AutoDAN vs Prompt-level / PAIR.\n- **B \u2014 Attacker strength** (3 ordered levels: low / mid / high), operationalized as search/query budget, calibrated per family (see \u00a73).\n- **C \u2014 Defense strength** (3 ordered levels): (C0) vanilla aligned baseline (no defense); (C1) **system-prompt guardrail** (fixed safety policy in the system prompt); (C2) **filtered/safety-tuned variant** (stronger safety-aligned model, or external output-filter wrapper e.g., a refusal/safety classifier). This matches your suggested operationalization.\n- **D \u2014 Previously tested tricks** (k\u22484\u20136 levels), prompt scaffolds crossed multiplicatively with the attacks, e.g., {control/no-trick, role-play/DAN persona, cross-language/encoding (e.g., base64 or translation), hypothetical/fictional-frame, authority/system-override}. Applied as a context scaffold around the seed prompt; for AutoDAN the genetic algorithm then optimizes a suffix on top of the trick scaffolded prompt. **Exact trick list is an uncertainty \u2014 needs your confirmation of what counts as \"previously tested\"** (see \u00a75).\n- **E \u2014 Dataset** (3 cross-levels): e.g., (i) AdvBench behaviors subset (category-balanced, sampled from the standard 50/520), (ii) JailbreakBench JBB-Behaviors, (iii) a third category-balanced set (HarmBench subset or a custom category-balanced sample). Fixed item lists per dataset.\n- **Replication**: each (A\u00d7B\u00d7C\u00d7D\u00d7E) cell run over the item set with R\u22655 attack seeds (stochastic optimization) to estimate within-cell ASR variance.\n\nFull factorial = 2\u00d73\u00d73\u00d7k\u00d73 cells\u00d7items\u00d7R. That is the skeleton; a concrete protocol would add downsizing (e.g., nested item sampling, possibly restricting D to a fixed subset, and a compute-feasibility pass) \u2014 flagged as a downstream optimization, not part of this plan.\n\n## 3. Measuring ASR and operationalizing the strength axes\n- **ASR measurement**: per-item binary success, judged by a **judge LLM with a fixed rubric** (does the target produce a useful, on-topic harmful/unsafe response, i.e., an *answer* rather than a refusal or off-topic/stalling answer?) plus a **secondary rule-based refusal heuristic** (\"I'm sorry\", \"I cannot\", \"As an AI...\", etc.) as a cross-check. Aggregate to proportion per cell; report bootstrap 95% CIs. Also record **refusal rate** and **evasive/off-topic rate** so we do not collapse ambiguous outputs into one binary.\n- **ASR thresholding**: pre-register the success criterion before judging; treat the judge rubric as primary and run sensitivity analyses with (i) stricter (requires explicit harmful content) vs (ii) lenient (any non-refusal following the behavior) thresholds; report whether headline interactions survive both.\n- **Attacker strength axis (B)**: *within-family* budgets: PAIR \u2192 number of refinement iterations (e.g., 3 / 10 / 20); AutoDAN \u2192 total suffix-evaluation budget on the target (e.g., \u2248100 / 500 / 2000 candidate-completions) chosen so per-level *target token/query cost is roughly matched across families* to make levels interpretable as comparable \"attacker budget\". Note cross-family exact matching is only approximate (queries vs token-evaluations) \u2192 uncertainty \u00a75.\n- **Defense strength axis (C)**: C1 = one fixed, verbatim safety-policy system prompt appended in the system role; C2 = either a documented safety-tuned checkpoint of the same base family or an external filter intercepting generations. Because defenses are not literally a continuum, C is treated as **ordered-categorical**, with a planned empirical check that ASR decreases monotonically in C (a non-monotonicity finding is itself a legitimate result).\n\n## 4. Interaction-analysis logic\n- Primary model: **mixed-effects logistic regression on per-item binary outcomes** (or equivalent cell-level ANOVA/regression on aggregated ASR), with effect coding:\n  `ASR_item ~ A*B*C*D + (1 | dataset-item) + (1 | seed)`, plus E as a fixed crossed factor (or a second random term) since the question demands \"across datasets\".\n- **Main effects**: B (does ASR rise with attacker budget?), C (does ASR fall with defense strength?), A (style main effect), D, E.\n- **Two-way interactions of interest**:\n  - B\u00d7C (headline): does stronger defense flatten/steepen the attacker-strength curve (diminishing returns of budget under high defense)?\n  - A\u00d7C: do defenses act differently on token-level vs prompt-level attacks (e.g., system-prompt guardrails may curb prompt-level attacks while being bypassed by token-suffix optimization)?\n  - A\u00d7B: which attack style needs more budget to saturate (rate-of-ascent differences)?\n  - C\u00d7D and D\u00d7A: which tricks erode specific defenses / transfer across styles.\n- **Three-way** (the \"interaction dynamics between attacker power and defense robustness\"):\n  - A\u00d7B\u00d7C: is the strength\u00d7defense curve itself moderated by attack style?\n  - B\u00d7C\u00d7D: which tricks shift the strength\u00d7defense trade-off?\n  - A\u00d7D\u00d7C: style-specific trick\u00d7defense behavior.\n- Reporting: marginal means + contrasts (linear/quadratic trend of B within each C level), interaction plots, bootstrap CIs, and a multiple-comparison correction across the interaction tests; a pre-registered analysis plan to keep the interaction mining principled.\n\n## 5. Evidence\n- Only source evidence available in-repo: `task.json` (reproduced above); no track.md, docs, datasets, or code exist in `/workspace` (confirmed by directory scan: `find /workspace` \u2192 task.json only; root scan \u2192 no other doc dirs; `pip list` \u2192 no ML/stats packages; Python 3.14, network disabled, GPU=false). Base design facts (AutoDAN token-level genetic suffix search; PAIR prompt-level iterative attacker; AdvBench-family datasets; judge-based ASR conventions) come from the public literature / the fixed facts you supplied.\n\n## 6. Consequential assumptions (what I'm carrying)\n1. \"Previously tested tricks\" = prompt scaffolds of the general type in \u00a72 (role-play, translation/encoding, hypothetical frame, authority override) \u2014 I am **assuming** the source's \"tricks\" are prompt-level scaffolds applicable in both attack styles.\n2. Attacker strength is proxied by **iteration/query budget**, calibrated so low/mid/high are comparable across families in target-query cost; attacker-model capability held fixed.\n3. Defense strength is an **ordered categorical**: C1 = system-prompt guardrail, C2 = safety-tuned/filtered variant; vanilla = C0.\n4. Datasets are the three candidate sets above (or the actual source-backed set, subject to confirmation), sampled category-balanced.\n5. ASR = binary judge-rubric success with a refusal-string cross-check; thresholding sensitivity handled as a secondary analysis.\n6. A mixed-effects logistic model with effect coding is an appropriate inference engine for the interaction logic.\n\n## 7. Uncertainties requiring your confirmation\n- Exact identity/taxonomy of the **\"previously tested tricks\"** (not present in-repo) \u2014 the biggest open point.\n- Whether defense level C2 should be a **different safety-tuned checkpoint** (confounds base-model capability with defense strength) vs an **external filter wrapper** (keeps the base model constant). I l